<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Short (half-class) telling-and-practice activity: antiderivatives of
     polynomials and particular antiderivatives.
     Arc: reverse the derivative by guess and check (Section 1), name the
     antiderivative and invert the power rule with a flow chart (Section 2),
     then use a data point to find the constant C (Section 3).

     Timing: aim for about 15 minutes of material, since this is meant to
     fill roughly half of a class period. If it runs long, trim in this
     order: Section 2 task 4 (the product), Section 3 task 3 (the position
     application), then Section 1 task 2 part c.

     Notation: the antidifferentiation operator is written in bracket form
     to match d/dx[ ], as in \int dx[ a x^n ]. The introduction of Section 2
     mentions the usual form \int a x^n dx so students can recognize it later.

     Convention: no data point in Section 3 uses x=0 (or t=0), so students
     do not conclude that C always equals the given data value. In each
     example C differs from the data value.

     Answer key
     Section 1 task 1: all three derivatives are 2x. Knowing only f'(x)=2x
       does not determine f(x). The candidates differ by a constant.
     Section 1 task 2: a. 3x^4   b. 2x^5   c. 7x.
     Section 1 task 3: the exponent goes up by 1. The coefficient is divided
       by the new exponent: 12/4 = 3, 10/5 = 2, 7/1 = 7 (7 = 7x^0 becomes 7x^1).
     Section 2 worked example: \int dx[ 12x^3 - 10x + 3 ] = 3x^4 - 5x^2 + 3x + C.
     Section 2 task 1: 8x^3 gives 2x^4 + C.
     Section 2 task 2: 6x^2 - 4x + 7 gives 2x^3 - 2x^2 + 7x + C.
     Section 2 task 3: 9x^2 - 5x + 4 gives 3x^3 - (5/2)x^2 + 4x + C.
     Section 2 task 4: (x+1)(x+3) = x^2 + 4x + 3 gives
       (1/3)x^3 + 2x^2 + 3x + C.
     Section 3 worked example: f'(x) = 6x^2 - 2, f(2) = 10.
       f(x) = 2x^3 - 2x + C, f(2) = 12 + C = 10, C = -2, f(x) = 2x^3 - 2x - 2.
     Section 3 task 1: f'(x) = 4x^3 - 6x, f(1) = 5.
       f(x) = x^4 - 3x^2 + C, f(1) = -2 + C = 5, C = 7, f(x) = x^4 - 3x^2 + 7.
     Section 3 task 2: f'(x) = 3x^2 + 4x - 1, f(-2) = 3.
       f(x) = x^3 + 2x^2 - x + C, f(-2) = 2 + C = 3, C = 1,
       f(x) = x^3 + 2x^2 - x + 1.
     Section 3 task 3: v(t) = 3t^2 - 4t, s(3) = 4.
       s(t) = t^3 - 2t^2 + C, s(3) = 9 + C = 4, C = -5,
       s(t) = t^3 - 2t^2 - 5. Then s(1) = 1 - 2 - 5 = -6 meters. -->
<worksheet xml:id="activity-polynomial-antiderivative">
  <title>Antiderivatives of Polynomials</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The derivative operator <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\ \right]</m> takes a function <m>f(x)</m> as its input and produces the derivative function <m>f'(x)</m>.
      If we know an object's position as a function of time <m>s(t)</m>, we can find a formula for its velocity <m>v(t)</m>, and then a formula for its acceleration <m>a(t)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Inverse operations are a major idea in mathematics, so we ask about the reverse process.
      If we know the derivative, can we find the original function?
      Knowing the acceleration, can we recover the velocity, and then the position?
      Today we learn to do this for polynomials.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-antideriv-explore" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 1: Running the derivative backward</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Finding a derivative follows a set of rules, and each rule can be run in reverse.
        We start by trying to undo a derivative with guess and check.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-same-derivative" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the derivative of each function.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p><m>f(x)=x^2+1</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)=x^2-4</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>f(x)=x^2+10</m></p></li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Suppose someone tells you only that <m>f'(x)=2x</m>.
          Can you tell which function <m>f(x)</m> they started with?
          Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-guess-check" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For each formula, find a function whose derivative is that formula.
          Check each guess by taking its derivative.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p><m>12x^3</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>10x^4</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>7</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-pattern" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Compare each formula in the previous task with the function you found for it.
          What happened to the exponent of <m>x</m>?
          What happened to the coefficient?
          (For part c, remember that <m>7=7x^0</m>.)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-antideriv-rule" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 2: The antiderivative of a polynomial</title>
    <introduction>
      <assemblage>
        <title>Antiderivative</title>
        <p>
          A function <m>F(x)</m> is an <term>antiderivative</term> of <m>f(x)</m> if <m>F'(x)=f(x)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The derivative of a constant is <m>0</m>, so if <m>F(x)</m> is an antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m>, then so is <m>F(x)+C</m> for any constant <m>C</m>.
          That is why you could not pin down <m>f(x)</m> in the first task.
          We cannot speak of <em>the</em> antiderivative, so we write the whole family of antiderivatives with an arbitrary constant <m>C</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>\int dx\left[ f(x) \right] = F(x) + C</mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        The symbol <m>\displaystyle\int dx\left[\ \right]</m> is the reverse of <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[\ \right]</m>.
        (Later courses write this same operation as <m>\int f(x)\,dx</m>.)
        To find the rule for a single term, we compare what the derivative does to <m>a x^n</m> with what the antiderivative must do to undo it.
        The derivative performs three steps, and the antiderivative reverses each one, in reverse order.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-antideriv-flowchart">
        <caption>Differentiating and antidifferentiating <m>a x^n</m></caption>
        <image xml:id="image-antideriv-flowchart" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>
            A flow chart with two rows of boxes. The top row, labeled derivative, starts with a x to the n,
            then step 1 keep the constant a, step 2 multiply by the power n, step 3 lower the power of x from n to n minus 1,
            and ends with a n x to the n minus 1. The bottom row, labeled antiderivative, starts with a x to the n,
            then step 3 prime raise the power of x from n to n plus 1, step 2 prime divide by the new power n plus 1,
            step 1 prime keep the constant a, and ends with a over n plus 1 times x to the n plus 1, plus C.
          </shortdescription>
          <latex-image>
            <![CDATA[
            \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=0.5cm,
                io/.style={draw, rounded corners=6pt, minimum height=1.3cm, minimum width=2.2cm, align=center, inner sep=3pt, fill=gray!12},
                proc/.style={draw, rectangle, minimum height=1.3cm, text width=2.2cm, align=center, inner sep=3pt, font=\footnotesize}]
              % Derivative row
              \node[font=\small\bfseries, anchor=south west] at (0,1.0) {Derivative: $\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ a x^n \right]$};
              \node[io] (d0) at (0,0) {$a\,x^n$};
              \node[proc, right=of d0] (d1) {\textbf{(1)} Keep the constant $a$};
              \node[proc, right=of d1] (d2) {\textbf{(2)} Multiply by the power $n$};
              \node[proc, right=of d2] (d3) {\textbf{(3)} Lower the power of $x$ from $n$ to $n-1$};
              \node[io, right=of d3] (d4) {$a\,n\,x^{n-1}$};
              \draw[->, thick] (d0) -- (d1);
              \draw[->, thick] (d1) -- (d2);
              \draw[->, thick] (d2) -- (d3);
              \draw[->, thick] (d3) -- (d4);
              % Antiderivative row
              \node[font=\small\bfseries, anchor=south west] at (0,-2.0) {Antiderivative: $\displaystyle \int dx\left[ a x^n \right]$};
              \node[io] (a0) at (0,-3.0) {$a\,x^n$};
              \node[proc, right=of a0] (a1) {\textbf{(3$'$)} Raise the power of $x$ from $n$ to $n+1$};
              \node[proc, right=of a1] (a2) {\textbf{(2$'$)} Divide by the new power $n+1$};
              \node[proc, right=of a2] (a3) {\textbf{(1$'$)} Keep the constant $a$};
              \node[io, right=of a3] (a4) {$\dfrac{a}{n+1}\,x^{n+1} + C$};
              \draw[->, thick] (a0) -- (a1);
              \draw[->, thick] (a1) -- (a2);
              \draw[->, thick] (a2) -- (a3);
              \draw[->, thick] (a3) -- (a4);
            \end{tikzpicture}
            ]]>
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <assemblage>
        <title>The Power Rule for Antiderivatives</title>
        <p>
          For a constant <m>a</m> and a power <m>n=0,1,2,\dots</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\int dx\left[ a x^n \right] = \frac{a}{n+1}\,x^{n+1} + C</mrow>
          </md>.
          Step <m>(1')</m> is the constant multiple rule, and the sum rule lets us work one term at a time.
          We add a single <m>+C</m> at the end, since a sum of constants is again an arbitrary constant.
          A constant term is a power with <m>n=0</m>, so <m>\displaystyle\int dx\left[ a \right] = a x + C</m>.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Checking an Antiderivative</title>
        <p>
          If <m>\displaystyle\int dx\left[ f(x) \right] = F(x)+C</m>, check your work by computing <m>\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[ F(x) \right]</m> and verifying that it matches <m>f(x)</m>.
          The <m>C</m> disappears when you differentiate.
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        Here is an example, with its check.
        <md>
          <mrow>\int dx\left[ 12x^3 - 10x + 3 \right] \amp = \frac{12}{4}x^4 - \frac{10}{2}x^2 + \frac{3}{1}x + C = 3x^4 - 5x^2 + 3x + C</mrow>
          <mrow>\frac{d}{dx}\left[ 3x^4 - 5x^2 + 3x \right] \amp = 12x^3 - 10x + 3 \quad\checkmark</mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Find each antiderivative, and check each answer by differentiating.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-practice-1" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \int dx\left[ 8x^3 \right]</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-practice-2" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \int dx\left[ 6x^2 - 4x + 7 \right]</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-practice-fractions" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \int dx\left[ 9x^2 - 5x + 4 \right]</m>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-practice-expand" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>\displaystyle \int dx\left[ (x+1)(x+3) \right]</m>
        </p>
        <p>
          We have no rule for a product, so what must you do to the formula first?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-antideriv-particular" workspace="0in">
    <title>Section 3: Finding a particular antiderivative</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Sometimes we know the formula for <m>f'(x)</m> and also one specific value of <m>f(x)</m>, such as <m>f(a)</m> at a particular <m>x=a</m>.
        The general antiderivative could have <em>any</em> constant added, but the data point singles out the one constant that matches.
      </p>

      <assemblage>
        <title>Finding a Particular Antiderivative</title>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Find the general antiderivative <m>f(x)=F(x)+C</m>, keeping the symbol <m>C</m> in the formula.</p></li>
            <li><p>Evaluate the formula at <m>x=a</m>, with <m>C</m> still present, to get <m>F(a)+C</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Set <m>F(a)+C</m> equal to the known value of <m>f(a)</m>, and solve the equation for <m>C</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write the final formula for <m>f(x)</m> using that particular value of <m>C</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </assemblage>

      <p>
        <em>Example.</em>
        Find <m>f(x)</m> if <m>f'(x)=6x^2-2</m> and <m>f(2)=10</m>.
        <md>
          <mrow>f(x) \amp = \int dx\left[ 6x^2 - 2 \right] = 2x^3 - 2x + C</mrow>
          <mrow>f(2) \amp = 2(2)^3 - 2(2) + C = 12 + C</mrow>
          <mrow>12 + C \amp = 10 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad C = -2</mrow>
          <mrow>f(x) \amp = 2x^3 - 2x - 2</mrow>
        </md>.
        Notice that <m>C=-2</m> is not the given value <m>10</m>.
        The data value is <m>f(2)</m>, which is the whole formula evaluated at <m>x=2</m>, and <m>C</m> is only one piece of it.
        As a check, <m>f(2)=16-4-2=10</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-particular-1" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(x)</m> if <m>f'(x)=4x^3-6x</m> and <m>f(1)=5</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-particular-2" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find <m>f(x)</m> if <m>f'(x)=6x^2+4x-1</m> and <m>f(-2)=3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-antideriv-particular-position" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          An object moves along a line with velocity <m>v(t)=3t^2-4t</m> meters per second.
          At time <m>t=3</m> seconds, its position is <m>s(3)=4</m> meters.
        </p>
        <ol marker="a.">
          <li><p>Find the position function <m>s(t)</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Where is the object at time <m>t=1</m> seconds?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>
</worksheet>
